&= 0 \\
&\Rightarrow d + c = 0
\end{align*}

Similarly, adding the second and fourth equations, we get:

\begin{align*}
b + a &= -ad+bc + ad-bc \\
&= 0 \\
&\Rightarrow b + a = 0
\end{align*}

So, we have:

\begin{align*}
d + c &= 0, \\
b + a &= 0
\end{align*}

This system of equations can be solved for the variables. Let's set:

\begin{align*}
d &= -c, \\
b &= -a
\end{align*}

Substituting these values into the original equation, we get:

\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix} a & -a \\ -c & c \end{pmatrix}^{-1} &= \begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{a+1} & \frac{1}{-a+1} \\ \frac{1}{-c+1} & \frac{1}{c+1} \end{pmatrix} \\
\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{a} & \frac{1}{-a} \\ \frac{1}{-c} & \frac{1}{c} \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{a+1} & \frac{1}{-a+1} \\ \frac{1}{-c+1} & \frac{1}{c+1} \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

Equating corresponding entries, we get:

\begin{align*}
